  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Kingerikiti Agricultural Marketing Cooperative Society Limited</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Smallholder coffee farming in Mbinga and Nyasa districts in the southern highlands in Tanzania dates back to the colonial days. The close to a century of coffee economy has had its challanges ranging from fluctuating coffee prices, changes in the cooperative policy and emergence of better yielding agricultural crops that have over time out competed coffee. However, the Kingerekiti farmers have remained resilient and still embrace their coffee as an important source of income, price flatuations not withstanding. Coffee has assured farmers of income through the season and into the next season as farmers get advance payments on delivery and subsequent payments as the coffee gets auctioned. It is this spread in earnings that has made farmers continue to grow coffee. However, as observed, coffee industry suffers from quality and quantity issues. The governance of farmer cooperatives has remained a nagging problem. Financial management and control of farmers money has also been a borne of contention. These are all capacity issues which may be addressed by USADF organizational assistance grant to Kingerekiti AMCOS. , The project will directly benefit 320 active cooperative members who sell their coffee through the primary cooperative society. In addition, it is assumed at least 4 members in each of the households of the 320 members depends on income from coffee. These are 1,280 beneficiaries per every year of USADF support to the AMCOS. As coffee production increases through the envisaged interventions, more employment is expected on the farms, among the input suppliers, at processing facilities</narrative>
  </description>
